/* book Worked example: Q: A 50 Ω lossless line ends in ZL = 100 + j50 Ω. On the Smith chart, find (a) zL, Γ and the VSWR, and (b) the position d and length ls of a short-circuited shunt stub that matches the load. Compare with the formula answer of 1.4.
   1. (a) zL = (100 + j50)/50 = 2 + j1. Γ = (zL − 1)/(zL + 1) = (1 + j1)/(3 + j1) = 0.4 + j0.2 = 0.447∠26.57°. Plot zL where the r = 2 circle meets the x = +1 arc.
   2. Draw the VSWR circle through zL, centred on the chart centre, radius 0.447. It cuts the positive real axis at r = 2.62, so VSWR = 2.62.
   3. (b) A shunt stub needs admittance. y = 1/z sits diametrically opposite zL: yL = 0.4 − j0.2. Its wavelengths-toward-generator (WTG) reading on the outer scale is 0.463λ.
   4. Move clockwise (towards the generator) along the VSWR circle until it meets the g = 1 circle. The first crossing is y = 1 + j1, at WTG 0.162λ.
   5. Distance: from 0.463λ to the end of the scale at 0.500λ is 0.037λ, then on to 0.162λ, so d = 0.037λ + 0.162λ = 0.199λ.
   6. The stub must supply b = −1. A short circuit on the admittance chart is y = ∞, the right-hand end, at WTG 0.250λ. Move clockwise along the outer circle (g = 0) to b = −1, at WTG 0.375λ: ls = 0.375λ − 0.250λ = 0.125λ.
   7. The second crossing, y = 1 − j1 at WTG 0.338λ, gives d = 0.037λ + 0.338λ = 0.375λ; its stub supplies b = +1 at WTG 0.125λ, so ls = 0.125λ − 0.250λ + 0.500λ = 0.375λ.
   8. Check: the formula route of 1.4 gave d = 0.1988λ with ls = 0.125λ, and d = 0.375λ with ls = 0.375λ. The chart agrees to the third decimal, which is as close as a printed chart can be read.
   Answer: zL = 2 + j1, Γ = 0.447∠26.57°, VSWR = 2.62. Stub at d = 0.199λ with a shorted stub of 0.125λ (or d = 0.375λ with 0.375λ), the same as the formula answer. */
