/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A microstrip is a strip conductor of width W on a dielectric substrate of thickness h, with a continuous ground plane on the other side.
   - A stripline is a strip buried in a homogeneous dielectric between two ground planes.
   - Field confinement improves as frequency rises.
   - For W/h ≥ 1, a standard approximate design pair is:
   - εr is the substrate relative permittivity (dimensionless); h is substrate thickness in metres; W is strip width in metres; εeff is the effective relative permittivity (dimensionless).
   - Z0 is characteristic impedance in ohms; 120π Ω ≈ 377 Ω; ln is the natural logarithm; W/h is dimensionless.
   - The design steps are simple.
   - Guessing can be skipped.
   - with A = (Z0/60)√((εr + 1)/2) + ((εr − 1)/(εr + 1))(0.23 + 0.11/εr).
   - For a narrow strip (W/h ≤ 1) use Z0 = (60/√εeff) ln(8h/W + W/(4h)) Ω, with 0.04(1 − W/h)² added inside the bracket of the εeff formula; do not use the wide-strip formula outside its range.
*/
